cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Bubblecut: a 360° video editor for research recordings"
message: "If you use this software in your research, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Davidsen
    given-names: Jacob
    affiliation: "Big Soft Video, Aalborg University"
    # Add your ORCID here, e.g.
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
repository-code: "https://github.com/codeslayer84/BubbleCut"
url: "https://www.bigvideo.aau.dk/"
abstract: >-
  Bubblecut is a desktop editor for 360-degree video, built for research
  recordings rather than for broadcast. It imports equirectangular footage,
  mono or stereo, and previews it as a sphere the analyst can look around in.
  Clips can be trimmed, split and reordered, and each can be reoriented so the
  viewer starts facing whatever matters in the room. Text cards can be placed
  anywhere in the sphere and timed on the timeline, then either burned into the
  picture or exported as a sidecar so they become objects that can be grabbed
  and moved in CAVA360VR. Painterly and anonymising filters run on the GPU, per
  clip, and can be saved as presets. FFmpeg performs the decoding and the
  hardware-accelerated encoding, and exported files carry Spherical Video V2
  metadata so they play correctly in headsets.
keywords:
  - 360-degree video
  - video analysis
  - qualitative research
  - virtual reality
  - anonymisation
  - equirectangular
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
